🌿🌺 A Contemplation — The Self We Seek Is Already Present

Spiritual practice prepares the mind; Self-knowledge removes the mistaken identity through which we overlook our own completeness. Steady abidance allows this understanding to inform the way we live.

The distinction is subtle and precious. A peaceful mind is a blessing, yet peace comes and goes. The consciousness in whose presence both peace and disturbance are known does not arrive with tranquillity or depart with agitation.

Likewise, purity belongs to the mind’s preparation. The Self has never been impure. Sattva brings clarity, gentleness and discernment, yet even this luminous quality belongs to nature. Self-knowledge reveals the consciousness that illumines every quality without becoming bound by it.

Scripture therefore does more than console us or encourage good conduct. Unfolded through the Guru’s teaching and carefully examined through reflection, it serves as a means of knowledge of our true identity. Its purpose is fulfilled when the error “I am only this limited body and mind” is understood and resolved.

Our contemplation must also enter ordinary life. Is learning making us humbler? Are desires losing their command over us? Can we listen patiently, serve sincerely and meet praise or criticism with greater discernment? These are valuable signs of maturity, though virtue or a quiet mood alone should not be mistaken for Self-knowledge.

The heart of this teaching is deeply reassuring: the Self is present before the search begins, throughout its struggles, and when its purpose is fulfilled. Every question, doubt and moment of understanding is already illumined by consciousness.

Brahman-rest is firm understanding of this ever-present truth. Duties continue; care for others continues. What loosens is the mistaken belief that our essential being is confined to the changing conditions of life.

Let us study with reverence, reflect with honesty and practise with patience. The mind needs preparation; mistaken understanding needs correction. The Self needs no improvement.

The seeker’s deepest fulfilment is the recognition: the fullness I sought has always been my own essential nature.

🙏 Hariḥ Om Tat Sat.
May this understanding flower as humility, compassion and freedom in daily life.
